# What never goes to the image builder.
#
# Two separate reasons, and the first one is the serious one.
#
# **Secrets.** The Dockerfile copies `apps/api` wholesale, and that directory
# holds `.env` -- the Supabase service role key, which reads and writes every
# row in the database ignoring all access rules, and the Stripe secret, which
# can move money. Without this file both are baked into the image, and an image
# is a thing that gets pushed to a registry and pulled by build systems. The
# Dockerfile's own comment claims nothing secret is baked in; this is what makes
# that true.
#
# **Size.** The build context is everything in this directory, sent to the
# builder before the first instruction runs. That is 2.9 GB here, almost all of
# it the Rust build output, for an image that needs about 250 KB of source. Left
# alone it turns every deploy into a multi-gigabyte upload.
#
# Credentials are handed to the running container as environment variables by
# the host instead, so they exist while the process runs and nowhere else.

# --- Secrets --------------------------------------------------------------
# Every form, at every depth. `.env.example` holds names and never values, but
# the image has no use for it either.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# --- Build output ---------------------------------------------------------
# 2.7 GB of compiled Rust for the desktop app, which this service never touches.
target/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/build/

# --- The rest of the monorepo ---------------------------------------------
# The API needs `apps/api` and `packages/shared`. Everything else is the desktop
# app, its tooling and its assets, none of which is copied by the Dockerfile --
# but all of which would still be uploaded as part of the context.
apps/desktop/
apps/browser-extension/
packages/ai/
packages/design/
packages/evals/
packages/ui/
assets/
scripts/
src-tauri/

# --- Development noise ----------------------------------------------------
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
**/.DS_Store
**/*.log
Cargo.toml
Cargo.lock
